#{[17592186069566 #edb/ref 1061 114986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069566 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a58fbae9-fa8d-5cc2-8b3b-97a9b645d318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069566 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1061 4236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8854423e-52a6-5077-ab8f-c9de25d23206" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1061 4237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac3a35ba-8b05-5a52-9f06-dc5add492619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1061 4242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a2dd26d-51b3-5cc1-8396-efeb5b1b8bb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1061 4244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3464306e-5c3f-55f3-9c10-87ab309dded2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069796 #edb/ref 1061 4245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069796 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3222e5cc-52d3-5afd-8468-ab3d87c483b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069796 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1061 5059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8accb58-b4d7-5dc8-9c5e-a5ca4f3588a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1061 6170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ecc30896-0712-5c97-a35e-e51485979c95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, perfect square trinomials have a leading coefficient that is something other than $1.$ For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4x^2+12x+9\n$$\n\nhas a leading coefficient of $4,$ and it is a perfect square trinomial because it is the square of the binomial $(2x+3)$ as we can see below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+2(2x)(3)+(3)^{2} & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial with a leading coefficient other than $1,$ we use the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a+b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $4x^2+12x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+12x+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(6x)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $2x$ and ${\\color{blue}3}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(2x\\cdot 3)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula. In this case, we have $a=2x$ and $b=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** This method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. But we will learn more general methods in the future." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1065 1597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5d43b5e8-672e-5cdd-b7a3-8de29e6552f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1064 "How can we factor a trinomial that has a negative leading coefficient, like\n\n$$\n-25y^2 + 20y - 4\\,?\n$$\n\n \nWe can reduce this to a more familiar problem by factoring out $−1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(25y^2 - 20y + 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}-20y+4 & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-20y+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(10y)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(5y\\cdot 2)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y-2)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(25{y}^{2}-20y+4) & = \\\\ -(5y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1065 1598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "757d1827-0402-5d3d-b8da-a4dc88c254bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1067 "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial With a Negative Leading Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1064 "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the square of a difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a-b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $9x^2-24x+16$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-24x+16 & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(12x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have $a=3x$ and $b=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** Once again, we note that this method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1065 1856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "609ce9b5-1b4e-5dfc-9d22-4ec6ea2d640b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1067 "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070020 #edb/ref 1064 "The **point-slope form** of a line that passes through any point $(x_1,y_1)$ with slope $m$ is given by \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m(x-x_1).\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, consider the straight line graph which passes through the points $(3,1)$ and $(5,5)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7838ffcb8f56ebffd384763ff761990e15031bdc5cdf3f0cc1f964ed69a4806.png)\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,1),$ so we can use $(x_1,y_1) = (3,1)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points.\n\nFirst, calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{5-1}{5-3}=\\frac{4}{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=2$ and the point $(x_1,y_1) = (3,1)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =2(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If we simplify this point-slope formula, we obtain the slope-intercept form of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =2(x-3) \\\\ y-1 & =2x-6 \\\\ y & =2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We could put $(x_1,y_1)=(5,5).$ In this case, the point-slope form will be different:\n\n$$\ny-5=2(x-5)\n$$\n\n \nBut the resulting simplified slope-intercept form will be the same:\n\n$$\ny = 2x-5\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070020 #edb/ref 1065 8695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070020 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "43f9e39e-fc27-549d-8ec4-b2bf4f375a33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070020 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186071349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186071350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186071351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186071352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186071353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071348 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071348 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071348 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186071349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071348 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4ff0f62d-b46f-5fed-abea-e9c339cbff76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071349 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071349 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071349 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "831b8a12-3240-5543-8f0b-63a066174430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071350 #edb/ref 1080 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071350 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071350 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e71abec1-af90-564e-96cc-65f749dff99a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071351 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071351 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071351 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f9bd14c8-f201-5c70-ba77-5489006197a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071352 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071352 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071352 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "30891c0a-e32e-5e99-9826-2629f5f5792b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071353 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071353 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186071353 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "424e6c76-ef65-55f4-bb2c-05129500785f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508171 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508174 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508179 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508187 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508204 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508205 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508207 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508210 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508214 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508215 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508227 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508233 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186508241 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073752 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5e79a91b-c479-53da-9f06-59c082fa4187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186073997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186073998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505001 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505002 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505004 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505005 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505044 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505050 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505053 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505059 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505062 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505067 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505080 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505081 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505084 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186505092 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b4352698-7af7-5d74-9c3a-0f951c3e6078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7809af08-83ac-5450-986d-2614a3fd22d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1061 197419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "413b9b90-7693-5568-a868-970271a5a258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1061 197420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99e9f36f-648c-5025-bfca-c63f6f77d9b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074659 #edb/ref 1061 197424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30b3f613-45c5-50c5-bd79-5c12149aea27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve, *without* using graphs, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=4 \\\\ x-2 &=y\\,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order to solve the system without a graph, we will need to combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation.\n\nOne way to combine the equations is called the **substitution method.** To use the substitution method, we substitute for one variable in terms of the other.\n\nFor instance, notice that we can make $y$ the subject of the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\color{red}y=x - 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $y$ into the first equation, we get an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+(x-2) & =4 \\\\ 2x-2 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3.$ But we also need to figure out what $y$ is. We can do this by substituting $x=3$ back into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-2 \\\\ y & =3-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 3$ and $y = 1$, which can be written as $\\left(3, 1\\right).$\n\n**Note:** Whenever we isolate the variable in one equation, we have to substitute it into the *other* equation. We cannot substitute a variable into the same equation it came from, because this will not give us any more information about the variable." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1065 8131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1e8cb2d-3d4e-5cb9-8947-2bd9deb11b89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083330 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083330 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1