86553503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110660 #edb/ref 1039 "Using the Power of Quotient Rule with Two Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110660 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110660 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "422bde20-bd83-5a20-b708-734b585ac256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110819 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110819 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110819 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110819 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110819 #edb/ref 1045 "The Power of Quotient Rule With Algebraic Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110819 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110819 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110819 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110819 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f40ffb7e-1747-5f74-b124-0acd55b121f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111591 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111591 #edb/ref 1061 3552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111591 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b35013c-5ff4-5a7c-99fa-9b6c887c8c75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111591 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111592 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111592 #edb/ref 1061 3553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111592 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8fb38c77-55f0-5928-8c35-70c3ff0de790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111592 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111593 #edb/ref 1061 3556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111593 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d9b9bca6-86d4-5640-8cb5-7659bf64aa00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111593 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111823 #edb/ref 1064 "The **power of quotient rule** states that if we want to raise the quotient $\\dfrac{x}{y}$ to some power $n,$ we must raise the numerator and denominator to the power $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^n=\\dfrac{x^n}{y^n}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{x^4}{y^4}.\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y} & = \\\\ \\frac{x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y\\cdot y\\cdot y} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111823 #edb/ref 1065 1484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111823 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9aedc7af-544c-5314-a986-2cd6ce64479b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111823 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Equations With Even Exponents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b936abcf-8e73-524c-a9bf-e59b55a48f5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Equations Containing an Expression Raised to an Even Power" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "23e661ed-3a7b-598a-a7b1-fd5011fe4fb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Equations With Even Exponents Using the Nth Root Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b0012b09-9794-5364-a20b-bf1c5e744a22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116226 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116226 #edb/ref 1061 30068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116226 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3cf6da35-b32f-503b-b8ea-94efb7441f1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116226 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116227 #edb/ref 1061 30120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116227 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c66f2f53-2687-55ab-8bb5-e1056e5074bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116227 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116230 #edb/ref 1061 30128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "036c0d84-9bf6-5da8-986c-2ace277257a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116534 #edb/ref 1064 "How do we solve an equation with a variable raised to an even exponent, such as\n\n$$\nx^6 = 64?\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by taking the $6$th root of each side and solving for $x.$ \n\nSince the exponent $(6)$ is even, there will be two real solutions (positive and negative):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{{x}^{6}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{64} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{{2}^{6}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nIn general, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n*When $n$ is even, and we take the $n$th root of both sides of $x^n = a$ with $a \\geq 0,$ we get two solutions (positive and negative):* \n\n$$\nx = \\pm\\sqrt[n]{a}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If $a<0$ and $n$ is even, equation  $x^n = a$ has no solution since, in this case, $x^n \\geq 0$ for any real $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116534 #edb/ref 1065 4833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116534 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6a9834f3-f04b-5340-b297-793072b594d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116534 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133849 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133849 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133849 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133849 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133849 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133849 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133849 #edb/ref 1039 "Interpreting the Meaning of the Coefficient and Constant Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133849 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133849 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ad5ad311-bf6f-58e9-a473-51ad651160fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133850 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133850 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921861338